## ABSTRACT
In  the  past  decades,  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  and  covalent  organic  frameworks  (COFs)  basically  enjoy  the coordination chemistry and covalent chemistry, respectively, and such uniqueness has become the major obstacle hampering their  further  scope  diversity  and  application  multi-functionalization.  Inspired  from  the  principle  of organic  retrosynthesis, combining coordination bond and covalent bond together offers additional opportunities for constructing novel MOFs, COFs and MOF@COF  hybrids  as  well  as  confer  on  them  superior  performances  in  versatile  application  fields.  In  this  review,  we  firstly classify  and  summarize  the  recently  reported  synthesis  strategies  based  on  the  integration  of  metal–ligand  coordination  and dynamic covalent bonds. Then, the application performances of as-constructed MOFs, COFs as well as MOF@COF hybrids are discussed  and  highlighted  in  the  fields  of  adsorption,  separation,  catalysis,  biosensing,  energy  storage  and  so  on.  Last,  our personal insights of the remaining challenges and further prospects are also provided, in order to trigger much more inspirations and endeavors for this hot research field.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs),  covalent  organic  frameworks  (COFs),  hybrids,  coordination  chemistry,  covalent chemistry, applications
## Introduction
Emerging as two types of novel crystalline porous materials, metalorganic  frameworks  (MOFs)  [1–3]  and  covalent-organic frameworks (COFs) [4–6] have drawn huge attentions thanks but not  limited  to  their  ultrahigh  surface  area,  well-ordered  and uniform pore structure and easy modification and functionalization.  Typically,  under  the  guidance  of  reticular chemistry  [7–10],  MOFs  are  constructed  via  the  coordination bond  between  metal  ions/secondary  metal  nodes  and  organic ligands  [11–14];  while  COFs  are  linked  via  the  covalent  bond between  different  organic  building  blocks  [15–18].  Such  specific bonding type of MOFs and COFs, however, has heavily hindered their further propagation of family members as well as realization of  multifunctionality  by  bonding  design.  Inspired  by  the retrosynthesis  concept  in  organic  chemistry,  a  target  product  is available from various synthesis routes involving specific synthons [19]. In principle, MOFs and COFs can also be disassembled into different  synthon  combinations  via  combining  the  chemistry  of both coordination and covalent bonds. For instance, several types of unusual MOFs [20–23] have been successfully constructed via in-situ formation of covalent bonds (e.g., imine and B–O bond) using  the  elaborately  designed  metal  nodes  and  organic  ligands. Meanwhile, it is also realizable to construct COF materials [24–26] with  the  aid  of  directional  metal–ligand  coordination  bonds. Moreover,  series  of  strategies  for  hybridization  of  MOFs  and COFs are boomingly developed recently [27–34], which have not only expanded the scope of crystalline porous materials but also integrated  both  advantages  of  MOFs  and  COFs  for  pursing enhanced performances in versatile applications.
Over past decades, MOFs have been extensively explored in gas storage  [35–37],  separation  [38–40],  catalysis  [41–49],  energy conversion  [50–54]  and  so  on  [55–64]  by  taking  properties  of both  inorganic  metal  nodes  and  organic  ligands.  Nonetheless, when  employed  in  practical  applications,  MOFs  generally  suffer from the susceptible chemical stability (e.g., inferior moisture, acid, and  base  stability)  [65, 66]  caused  by  the  relatively  weak metal–ligand coordination bond. On the contrary, COFs exhibited robust  chemical  stability  beneficial  for  maintaining  long-term stable  performance  [67, 68],  but  the  limited  scope  of  covalent bonds  used  for  construction  of  COFs  has  seriously  obstructed their type diversity and multi-functionalization. Get two birds with one stone, novel porous materials via integrating both MOF and COF  chemistries  have  inherited  both  advantages  and  even surpassed  the  performance  of  corresponding  individuals  in  the application  of  adsorption  [69, 70],  separation  [71–73],  catalysis [74–79] and so on [80–85].
In  this  review,  we  firstly  summarize  the  strategies  for construction of new MOF and COF materials via simultaneously combining  the  chemistry  of  coordination  and  covalent  bonds. Also,  the  emerging  protocols  for  hybridization  of  MOFs  and COFs have been presented. Thanks to the integrated properties, asconstructed  MOFs,  COFs,  and  MOF@COF  hybrids  are  then highlighted and discussed in the application fields of adsorption, separation,  catalysis  and  so  on.  Last  but  not  least,  our  personal insights  concerning  on  the  remaining  challenges  and  future advancing are also provided. We hope this comprehensive review will not only be helpful for rational design and synthesis of novel MOF-  or/and  COF-based  multifunctional  materials  but  also further expanding their application visions in the near future.
## 2    Synthetic strategies for combining MOFs and COFs
## 2.1    COFs inspired synthesis of MOFs
Due to the incompatibility between metal nodes and conventional synthesis  conditions  of  MOFs  (e.g.,  the  indissolubility  and susceptibility  of  metal  nodes  at  the  synthesis  conditions),  it  is normally difficult to directly use metal nodes as starting precursors for  constructing  MOFs  with  new  structures  and  functionalities. Fortunately,  COF  inspired  synthesis  strategies  bring  additional opportunities  for  construction  of  unusual  MOFs  via in-situ formation of dynamic $\mathrm{C=N}$ and B–O bonds, which require prefunctionalized  metal  clusters  as  well  as  elaborately  designed organic ligands. For example, Yaghi and co-workers [20] reported the  synthesis  of  a  new  type  of  Ti-MOF  (MOF-901)  featuring unusual  hexameric  titanium  oxo $\mathrm{(Ti_{6}O_{6})}$ metal  node  via  the dynamic  imine  condensation.  In  specification,  the  reaction precursors  containing  titanium(IV)  isopropoxide $\mathrm{(Ti(O^{\prime}P r)_{4})}$ ,  4- aminobenzoic  acid  (H-AB),  and  benzene-1,4-dialdehyde  (BDA) dissolved in methanol were solvothermally reacted in a sealed tube under inert nitrogen atmosphere at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days. During the synthesis  process,  the in-situ formed $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{6}(\mathrm{AB})_{6}$ metal node was further linked with BDA ligand via the reversible imine condensation  to  construct  the  final  MOF-901  product  in  good crystallinity (Fig. 1(a)). Moreover, the proposed strategy was also feasible  for  changing  BDA  to $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxaldehyde (BPDA)  in  the  case  of  construction  of  MOF-902  under  similar solvothermal treatments [86]. Noteworthily, these are the only two Ti-MOFs consisting unusual $\mathrm{Ti}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ octahedron metal node.
Polyoxometalates (POMs) are a kind of classical metal clusters characteristic of structural and property diversity [87–89]. Yaghi and  co-workers  further  extended  the  COF  inspired  synthesis strategy to build POM-based MOFs, known as MOF-688 [23]. In details,  the  amino  functionalized  Anderson  type  POM $[\mathrm{(N(C_{4}H_{9})_{4}]_{3}[M n M o_{6}O_{18}\{(O C H_{2})_{3}C N H_{2}\}_{2}]}$ (denoted as $\mathrm{MnMo}_{6}^{3-}$ ) was  chosen  as  the  metal  node  for  linking  the  4-connected tetrahedral  tetrakis(4-formylphenyl)methane  (TFPM)  ligand  via imine  condensation.  Thanks  to  the  introduction  of  highly negatively charged $\mathrm{MnMo}_{6}^{3-}$ cluster, MOF-688 was competent for abundant installation of lithium ions and thereby giving high ionic conductivity up to $3.4\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Recently, more POMbased  MOFs  have  been  successfully  constructed  via  the  same imine  condensation  between  amino  functionalized  POM  and aldehyde type building units [90–92].
In  addition,  Li  and  co-workers  [22]  successfully  developed  a catalytically active $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ cyclic trinuclear unit (CTU) based MOFs by utilizing reversible imine bond. Typically, $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}$ , $1H.$ -pyrazole-4- carbaldehyde  ligand  (HL),  and,  3,5-tris(4-aminophenyl)benzene (1) or $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -(1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6- triyl)trianiline (2) were mixed in the  mesitylene/dioxane/aqueous  acetic  acid  solution,  and  the resultant mixture was kept at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the JNM-1 $\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}\mathbf{1}_{3}\right)$ or JNM-2 $\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}\mathbf{2}_{3}\right)$ , respectively (Fig. 1(b)). Moreover, JNM-1  or  JNM-2  could  also  be  obtained  by  reacting  the  preconstructed aldehyde functionalized $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}$ with organic linker 1 or 2 via  a  stepwise  synthesis  strategy.  Li’s  group  [93]  reported FDM-71 $\mathrm{\dot{F}D M=}$ Fudan  materials)  by  via $\mathrm{Cu-N}$ coordination. Then,  the  treatment  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{PyCA})_{3}$ $\mathrm{(PyCA~=~}$ pyrazolate-4- carboxaldehyde) cluster with p-phenylenediamine (PA) under the existence  of  minute  quantity  of  water  at $170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led  to  the  final production  of  FDM-71.  In  another  case,  Murugavel  et  al.  [94] successfully constructed series of novel crystalline zinc phosphatebased frameworks. Thereinto, four aldehyde groups dangling on the  vertex  of  elaborately  designed  tetrahedron $\mathrm{(Zn_{4}(d i p p)_{4}(4-P y-}$ $\mathrm{CHO})_{4})$ (dipp $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ diiminopyridine,  and $4{\cdot}\mathrm{Py}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CHO}=4$ -pyridine carboxaldehyde)  cluster  were  condensed  with  different  types  of linear diamines via imine condensation.
As glorious stars in the MOF family, zirconium-based MOFs (Zr-MOFs) have exhibited great potentials in practical applications due  to  their  robust  stability,  adjustable  porosity,  structural diversity,  etc.  [95].  When  being  introduced  into $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs,  the imine-based  linkers  are  prone  to  be  decomposed  under  the required  harsh  solvothermal  conditions  (e.g.,  strong  acidity  and high  reaction  temperature)  [96].  Farha  and  co-workers  [97] proposed a COFs inspired synthesis strategy for in situ formation of imine-based $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs. In the solvothermal reaction system, 4- aminobenzoic acid or $\boldsymbol{p}$ -phenylenediamine, 4-formylbenzoic acid and $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}$ were separately dissolved in N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF). Then, the mixed solution containing above two types of precursors was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to yield the light-yellow NU-401 or NU-402, respectively. In which, the monocarboxylates functionalized with amino and aldehyde group not only acted as the building block for in situ formation of the imine-based linker, but  also  was  utilized  as  acid  modulator  for  improving  the crystallinity  of  MOF  products.  A  similar  design  and  synthetic method was adopted by Kaskel’s group [98, 99], who synthesized a  series  of  unconventional $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs  containing  imine-based linkers. In specification, the in situ formed dicarboxylate ligands via the imine bond (i.e., $\mathrm{CBAB}=4\cdot$ -carboxybenzylidene-4-amino benzoic  acid, ${\mathrm{CBHB}}\ =\ 4-$ (4-carboxyphenyl)-3-hydroxybenzoic acid, $\begin{array}{r l r}{{\bf L}{\bf4}^{\prime}}&{{}=}&{4,4^{\prime}.}\end{array}$ -((1E,1'E)-(1,4-phenylenebis(azanylylidene))- bis(methanylylidene))dibenzoic  acid)  were  further  coordinated with  classical $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters  to  form  the  PCN-161,  DUT-133 $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(C B H B)_{6}]}}$ and  PCN-164,  respectively.  In  another work reported from the same group, a novel chiral diimine DUT136 $\mathrm{(Zr_{6}O_{6}(L)_{3}(C H_{3}C O O)_{6},}$ $\mathrm{~L~}=\ 4\mathrm{,}4^{\prime}\mathrm{.}$ -(((1,2-diphenylethane-1,2- diyl)bis(azaneylylidene))bis(methaneylylidene))-dibenzoic acid)) was  successfully  synthesized  following  the  same  strategy  [99]. During the synthesis progress, the chiral diimine ligand was in situ formed by the condensation of 4-formylbenzoic acid with (R,R)- 1,2-diphenylethylenediamine.  Immediately,  the  formed  diimine ligand was further reacted with the $Z\mathrm{r}$ precursor for formation of the target DUT-136 in good crystallinity, which well addressed the poor  solubility  of  chiral  diimine  ligand  in  the  reaction  solution. Moreover,  the  hydrophobicity  and  steric  hindrance  of  the introduced diphenylethylenediamine group in the chiral diimine ligand effectively reduced the risk of the competitive hydrolysis of formed imine-based linker, which ensured the production of DUT136 in high yield.
In addition to the in-situ formation of the imine-based ligand, Zhou’s group proposed to install the labile imine-based linkers via a linker-exchange method [100, 101]. As a typical representation, they  successfully  constructed  a  series  of  non-interpenetrated isoreticular $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs  via  sequentially  linker-exchanging  [102]. First of all, UiO-67.5 was directly constructed by the classical $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster  and  azobenzene-based  linker  L1  (azobenzene- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic  acid).  Then,  the  parent  UiO-67.5  was  employed  for production  of  PCN-161  via  linker-exchange  of  L1  with  L1'  (4- carboxybenzylidene-4-aminobenzoic acid) of the same dimensional length. Interesting, owing to the labile nature of the imine-liker  in  PCN-161,  liker-exchange  between  linkers  with different  lengths  can  also  be  successfully  realized.  For  example, PCN-162–PCN-164  with  a  longer  linker  size  featuring progressively increased pore dimension were obtained in sequence via dissociation of the pristine imine-based linker in PCN-161 and subsequent reinstallation of longer imine-based linker. In addition, PCN-162–PCN-164  with  a  long  linker  size  tend  to  form interpenetrated  structures;  however,  the  corresponding  noninterpenetrated MOFs were obtained. The method also applicable for  further  construction  of  the  non-interpenetrated  UiO-68  and UiO-69  via  similar  linker-exchange  processes  (Fig. 2(a)).  It  is worthy to mention that PCN-163 and PCN-164 as well as UiO-68 and UiO-69 are only reported in the interpenetrated structures via
conventional one-pot syntheses.
Besides the imine bond, B–O linkage is another type of covalent bonds  and  has  also  been  widely  utilized  for  constructing  COF examples [103–105]. To achieve the goals of introducing dynamic B–O  bonds  into  MOFs,  in  2019,  Feng  and  coworkers  [21] reported  a  series  of  pore-space pacs-type  CPMs  (pacs $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ partitioned acs net,  and  CPMs $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ crystalline  porous  materials), which were ultimately constructed via the coordination between in  situ formed acs net  framework $((\mathrm{M1})_{1+x}(\mathrm{M2})_{2-x}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}$ $\left(\operatorname{L1}\right)_{3})$ $\mathbf{\Delta}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{l}=\mathbf{I}\mathbf{n}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ ; $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}2=\ensuremath{\mathbf{C}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ ; $\mathrm{L}1=1{,}4$ -terephthalic acid (BDC)) and 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridinyl)-1,3,5-boroxine trimer linker deriving  from  the  self-condensation  reaction  of  pyridine-4- boronic acid (Fig. 2(b)). In details, M1, M2, BDC, and pyridine-4- boronic acid were dissolved in the mixture of DMF and toluene in a sealed glass vial at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for 3 days to obtain the target CPM-100. The formation mechanism of pacs-type CPMs is that the metal-carboxylate trimer $(\left(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}\right)$ of the acs MOF-type framework is formed concurrently with the COF-type 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridinyl)- $^{.1,3,5}$ -boroxine (tbp) trimer via selfcondensation of pyridine-4-boronic acid. Furthermore, the metal ion of $(\left(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{1-x}(\mathrm{O})_{x}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}\right)$ was coordinated with the N site in the pyridine through coordination bonds to form the final pacstype  CPMs.  Li’s  group  [106]  developed  a  one-pot  method  for constructing  novel  B-O  linked ${\mathrm{Zr}}({\mathrm{Hf}}){\mathrm{-MOFs}}.$ ,  namely  BUT-108 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ $\left(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{L}8)_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right)$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}L8}=4{,}4{,}4{^{\prime}}{,}4{^{\prime\prime}}.$ -(1,3,5,2,4,6- trioxa-triborinane-2,4,6-triyl)tribenzoate)  and  BUT-108  (Hf) $\mathrm{(Hf_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(L8)_{2}(H C O O)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{2})}$ . The cyclotrimerized boronic acid linker L8 was in situ formed by the self-condensation reaction of 4-boronobenzoic acid and was subsequently coordinated with $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ clusters, respectively.
## 2.2    Metal–ligand  coordination  bonds  assisted  synthesis of COFs
Dating  back  to  1983,  Sauvage  and  co-workers  introduced  metal coordination bond as a template for formation of catenanes and rotaxanes [107]. In their seminal work, the predictable tetrahedral coordination geometry of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ ion was exploited to arrange two phenanthroline ligands perpendicular to each other for obtaining the catenane product. Moreover, Nitschke and co-workers [108] conceptually  showed  the  reversible  disassembly–reassembly system  constructed  via  the  directional  coordination  bonds. Analogously, the employment of metal–ligand coordination bond can  also  be  helpful  for  synthesis  of  COFs,  which  can  not  only improve  the  crystallinity  of  resulting  COF  examples  but  also confer  on  them  interesting  mechanical  and  dynamic  absorption properties.  As  a  semi-empirical  rule,  the  synthesis  strategy  of woven COF materials relied on functionalized amino or aldehyde organic ligand that was potentially available for coordination with additional metal ions. The metal ions serve as points of registry for arranging  the  mutually  interlaced  COF  threads  in  a  precisely weaving  manner.  Hence,  the  metal–ligand  coordination  bonds shall  be  stable  under  acid  conditions  normally  required  by  the synthesis of imine-based COF materials. In 2016, Yaghi and coworkers [24] introduced metal–ligand coordination for directing  syntheses  of  various  types  of  crystalline  woven  COFs. Taking  an  example,  the  woven $\mathrm{COF-}505(\mathrm{Cu})$ was  synthesized in  a  solvothermal  reaction  system  via  linking  the  aldehyde functionalized $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -bis[4,4'-(1,10-phenanthroline-2,9-diyl)dibenzaldehyde]tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(Cu(PDB)_{2}(B F_{4}))}$ with benzidine (BZ) (Fig. 3(a)). The successful formation of $\mathrm{COF-}505(\mathrm{Cu})$ with woven structure  was  dominated  by  the  imine  condensation  between aldehyde  group  armed $\mathrm{Cu(PDB)}_{2}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})$ building  block,  which was  pre-synthesized  via  a  directional  coordination  linkage between $\mathrm{[Cu(CH_{3}C N)_{4}]B F_{4},}$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(1,10-phenanthroline-2,9-diyl)- dibenzaldehyde  (PDB),  and  the  benzidine  (BZ)  linker  under  a weak acidic condition. Noteworthily, the $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ not only acted as the  point  of  registry  for  directing  the  ordered  woven in  situ formed  threads  by  interlacing  PDB  units,  but  also  could  be reversibly  removed  after  formation  of  the  woven  framework without  destroying  the  overall  structure.  Compared  with  the previous  COFs,  the  woven  COFs  show  obvious  advantages  in structural elasticity and reversibility. The effective Young’s moduli (neglecting  the  anisotropy  of  the  elasticity)  of  the  demetalated COF-505 and COF-505(Cu) materials were $\sim12.5$ and $1.3\mathrm{\GPa},$ respectively.  Notably,  this  ten-fold  ratio  in  elasticity  upon demetalation  of  COF-505  is  similar  to  the  elasticity  ratio  for porous MOFs to polyethylene. Moreover, the copper(I) ions can be  reversibly  removed  and  added  without  loss  of  the  COF structure.  However,  due  to  the  highly  interpenetrated  structure that  caused  small  internal  void  space,  the  loading  capabilities  of both  COF-505 $\mathrm{\mathop{Cu}})$ and  COF-505  towards  external  guests  were heavily inhibited. To resolve this issue, Yaghi’s group [110] further designed  the  non-interpenetrated  woven  COF-506(Cu),  which was  prepared  by  replacing  the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ with  the  bulky diphenylphosphinate $(\mathrm{PO}_{2}\mathrm{Ph}^{2-})$ under  solvothermal  reaction conditions similar to COF-505(Cu). Surprisingly, the demetalated COF-506 exhibited a novel mode of motional dynamics, known as “adaptive inclusion”, which enabled the uptake of guest molecules lager than its intrinsic pore size.
Following those, Yaghi and co-workers [111] also constructed a novel  mechanical  interlocking  framework, $\mathrm{COF}{-}500(\mathrm{Cu})$ ,  with  a pts topology.  In  particular,  the  aldehyde  functionalized $\mathrm{Cu(PDB)_{2}P O_{2}P h_{2}}$ as  the  building  block  was  linked  with  the  4- connected rectangular tetrahedral $4^{\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime\prime\prime}{,}4^{\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime\prime}$ -(ethene-1,1,2,2- tetrayl)tetrakis- $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]-4-amine) (ETTBA) ligand via imine condensation  reaction  to  form  the  crystalline  porous  COF$500(\mathrm{Cu})$ . The imine bond between the PDB unit and the ETTBA ligand  made  a  four-membered  cycle,  termed  as  corner-sharing one-dimensional  (1D)  square  ribbons,  which  were  interlocked with  each  other  to  form  a  3D  woven  framework.  Interestingly, $\mathrm{COF}{-}500(\mathrm{Cu})$ could  be  also  demetallized  to  transform  into  a mechanical  interlocking  COF-500  framework  featuring  a  high degree  of  moving  freedom.  Moreover,  immersing  COF-500 into the solution of acetonitrile/chloroform containing $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C N)_{4}B F_{4}}$ could  obtain  the  crystalline  COF-500(Cu) reversibly.
Additionally, the same group [109] reported a novel 3D noninterpenetrated woven COF-112 with a dia topology, which was formed  by  interweaving  1D  covalent  organic  thread.  In specification, $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ,  4-(tert-butoxy  carbonylamino)- aniline  (NBPDA)  and  2,6-pyridinedicarboxal-dehyde  (PCBA)
were  mixed  in  acetonitrile/toluene/aqueous  trifluoroacetic  acid (TFA)  solution,  and  the  resultant  mixture  was  kept  at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ .  During  the  synthesis  process,  the in  situ formed $\mathrm{Co(NHBoc-DIP)_{2}(B F_{4})_{2}}$ ( $\mathbf{Boc}=$ tert-butyloxycarbonyl, and $\mathrm{DIP=}$ bis(diiminopyridine)) complex was deprotected by TFA and the released  amnio  group  was  further  linked  with  the  remaining PCBA by imine condensation reaction, finally forming the COF112 product (Fig. 3(b)). It was meaningful to highlight the key role of  using  Boc-protected $\mathrm{Co(NHBoc-DIP)_{2}(B F_{4})_{2}}$ complex  as  the building  block,  which  avoided  the  formation  of  polyimide precipitates  before  that  the  DIP  sites  were  fully  coordinated  by cobalt ions.
Recently, Loh and co-workers [26] developed a novel strategy for the synthesis of 3D woven COFs by combining metal–ligand coordination and dynamic covalent chemistry together. First of all, two  bisphenanthroline  derivatives, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(1,10-phenanthroline-2,9 diyl)dianiline  (I)  and  2,9-bis(4-(dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10- phenanthroline  (II)  were  mixed  with $\mathrm{AgBF_{4}}$ in  1-butanol/1,2- dichlorobenzene/aqueous  acetic  acid  solution,  and  the  mixture was placed at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days to obtain single-crystalline metalloCOF  (mCOF-Ag).  Then,  the  free  amino  groups  among  the mCOF-Ag  could  be  further  crosslinked  with  glyoxal  by  imine condensation reaction to construct a novel 3D woven framework, namely  wCOF-Ag  (Fig. 4(a)).  Powder  X-ray  diffraction  (PXRD) showed that there were almost no changes in crystallinity before and after the cross-linking reaction. To one’s delight, the Young’s modulus  of  crosslinked  wCOF-Ag  was  significantly  improved. The same research group further extended the synthesis strategy for  construction  of  a  3D  woven  COF-Cu  [112].  Detailedly, amine functionalized $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{I}){\cdot}\operatorname{bis}[4,4^{\prime}$ -(1,10-phenanthroline-2,9- diyl)dianiline]tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(Cu(PDB)_{2}(B F_{4}))}$ and acetal functionalizedCu(I)-bis[2,9-bis(4-(dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10- phenanthroline]tetrafluoroborate $\left(\mathrm{Cu(PDB-OCH_{3})_{2}(B F_{4})}\right)$ building blocks were reacted under solvothermal conditions to obtain the 3- fold  interpenetrated  wCOF-Cu  with  a dia topology  (Fig. 4(b)). Specifically, the 1D metallo-COFs-Cu (mCOFs-Cu) was obtained by linker exchange of PDB with 2,9-bis(4- (dimethoxymethyl)phenyl)-1,10-phenanthroline $(\mathrm{PDB-OCH}_{3})$ 1 under solvothermal conditions similar to synthesis of wCOF-Cu. Interestingly,  the  hydrogen  bonding  between  amine  groups  and $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions and the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of adjacent phenanthroline rings both contributed greatly for increasing the mechanical strength of the resultant woven framework.
## 2.3    Synthesis of MOF and COF hybrids
Currently, numerous studies have shown that MOF-based hybrid materials could afford multifunctional properties via hybridization with  other  functional  materials  (e.g.,  metal  nanoparticles [113–116],  polymers  [117, 118],  and  other  MOFs  [119, 120]. Similarly, combining functional components with COFs to obtain COF-based  hybrid  materials  has  also  attracted  widespread concerns  [121–126].  Recently,  researchers  have  successfully achieved  the  hybridization  of  MOFs  and  COFs,  which  can  be further divided into two categories: $\mathrm{MOF@COF}$ hybrid materials synthesized by introducing the prepared MOFs into the synthetic system  of  COFs  and $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ hybrid  materials  prepared  via the growth of additional MOFs outside/inside the pre-synthesized COFs.  Accordingly,  the  additional  covalent  bonding  and metal–ligand coordination linking the two components together shall be also compatible with the synthesis conditions of hybrids. In 2018, Zhang’s group [27] proposed a novel $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–68@\mathrm{TPA}$ - COF  core–shell  material  by  hybridizing  MOFs  with  COFs  (Fig. 5(a)). Specifically, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 [127] was synthesized and further modified  with  tris(4-formylphenyl)amine  (TFPA),  one  of  the structural units of TPA-TFPA-COF [128], to prepare the TFPA modified  aldehyde-functionalized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}68(\mathrm{CHO})$ .  After charging of tris(4-aminophenyl) amine (TAPA), target TPA-COF was  grafted  on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68  homogeneously  via  imine condensation  reaction.  Following  that, $\operatorname{MOF@COF}$ hybrid materials  boomingly  emerged.  For  instances,  Qu’s  group  [129] reported  a  biomimetic $\mathrm{MOF@COF}$ hybrid  nanozyme.  At  first, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and  2-aminoterephthalic  acid $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC)  were dissolved in DMF, and the resulting mixture was heated at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to produce $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ . Then, the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-88B(Fe) was modified with 3,5-triformylphloroglucinol (Tp). Finally, $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}.}$ -(1,3,5-triazine- $^{2,4,6}$ -triyl)trianiline (TTA) was added  for  processing  the  condensation  reaction  to  prepare  the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{COF}_{\mathrm{TP-TTA}}$ hybrid  material.  Samorì  and  coworkers  [130]  fabricated  an  aza-MOF@COF  hybrid  porous material by the post-synthetic modification of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-UiO}}{\mathrm{-}}66@{\mathrm{COF}}{\mathrm{-}}$ LZU1  via  the  aza-Diels-Alder  cycloaddition  reaction  [131, 132]. Particularly, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 was prepared by reacting $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC in DMF at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Next, the aldehyde armed $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66(CHO)  was  obtained  after  modification  of  1,3,5- benzenetricarboxaldehyde (TFB), and was further reacted with $\boldsymbol{p}$ - phenylenediamine  (PDA)  to  form  a  COF-LZU1  layer  on  the surface  of  parent $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66.  Finally, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COFLZU1,  phenylacetylene,  boron  trifluoride  diethyl  etherate $(\mathrm{BF}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OEt}_{2})$ ,  and  chloranil  were  added  into  toluene,  and  the mixture was heated under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1–3 days to obtain the aza-MOF@COF hybrid structure.
Besides,  Kim  group  reported  [133]  an  interfacial  control strategy  for  concise  and  convenient  construction  of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL$125\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{LZU}1$ hybrid  material  free  of  tedious  functionalization steps. Briefly, the pre-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ $\mathrm{(Ti_{8}O_{8}(O H)_{4}(N H_{2}{-}B D C)_{6})}$ was mixed with TFB and PDA in 1,4- dioxane solution containing acetic acid, and the resulting mixture was sealed for 3 days at room temperature to produce the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{LZU1}$ (Fig. 5(b)).  Similarly,  Li’s  group  [134] developed  a  stable  core–shell $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{NTU}$ hybrid material  by  a  simple  one-step  method.  That  was,  4- formylphenylboronic acid (4-FPBA) and $1{,}3{,}5{\cdot}\mathrm{tris}(4{-}$ aminophenyl)-benzene (TAPB) as two kinds of building units of NTU-COF  were  mixed  with  the  pre-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL101(Fe) $\mathrm{(Fe_{3}O(H_{2}O)_{2}C l(N H_{2}{-}B D C)_{3}}.$ ) suspension to obtain $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-101(Fe) $@$ FPBA-TAPB-COF. By adjusting the added ratio of TAPB  to  4-FPBA,  the  formation  of  imine  bonds  and  boroxine rings  can  be  varied,  allowing  NTU-COF-1  to  be  controllably wrapped on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe). More hybridizations of MOFs and COFs have been reported via the similar one-pot strategy [69, 70, 85, 135].
In  addition,  Zhao’s  group  [136]  constructed  the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@\mathrm{TpPa-1}$ hybrid  material  by  utilizing  an  amorphous  polymer intermediated  method.  As  the  first  step,  Tp  and $\mathsf{p}\mathrm{-}$ phenylenediamine $\mathrm{(Pa)}$ were added to the pre-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - UiO-66 $\mathrm{(Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(N H_{2}\mathrm{-BDC)_{12})}}$ suspension. As a result, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - UiO-66 nanoparticles encased with an amorphous polyimine layer were obtained after the reaction at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As the second step,  the  resultant $\mathrm{MOF}@$ polyimine  was  dispersed  in  a mesitylene/1,4-dioxane mixture, which was subsequently incubated  at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for  3  days  in  order  to  accomplish  the transformation  of  amorphous  polyimine  to  crystalline  TpPa-1. Moreover,  Maspoch’s  group  [137]  synthesized  a  type  of microspherical $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA composite bead by a similar strategy. Firstly, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 nanocrystals were successfully  encapsulated  into  amorphous  imine-based  TAPBBTCA $(\mathrm{TAPB}=1,3,5 $ -tris-(4-aminophenyl)benzene, BTCA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=1,3,5.$ benzenetricarbaldehyde)  polymer  beads  through  spray  drying. Secondly, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@ploymer  beads  were  transformed  to target $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA under dynamic covalent chemistry conditions. That was, the beads were dispersed into a mixture of 1,4-dioxane and mesitylene (9:1 volume/volume), and then water and acetic acid were added to the resulting dispersion, which was heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the crystalline $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA.  Moreover,  Zhao’s  group  [29] prepared  the $\mathrm{Pd/NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-1  hybrid  material  in  a sandwich  fashion  (Fig. 5(c)).  First, $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66  prepared  by the  typical  wet  impregnation  strategy  was  mixed  with  1,4- phthalaldehyde  to  obtain  the  aldehyde-functionalized $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - UiO-66(CHO). Subsequently, $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66(CHO) was connected  with  TTA  to  form  amorphous $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66@$ Polymer.  Then,  by  replacing  1,4-phthalaldehyde  with  2,5- dihydroxytereph-thalaldehyde linker, the Pd/UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ polymer underwent crystallization transformation with the assistance of acetic acid at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3, 4, 5, and 6 days to obtain $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-n ( $\mathrm{COF-n}=\mathrm{COF}{-}3\mathrm{d}$ , COF-4d, COF-5d, COF-6d,  respectively)  hybrid  material.  More  successful  cases including $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{-}125\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{DM}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{I}$ ZU1  [138]  (DM-LZU1 $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dimethylated  COF-LZU1),  and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{DM}.$ LZU1  with  sandwich  structure  have  been  offered  via  utilizing  a similar strategy.
Not  merely  growing  COFs  onto  the  surface  of  MOFs, alternatively, the study of $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ hybrids has been proposed in recent years [30, 69, 139, 140]. For example, Lan’ s group [28] utilized  a  one-step  strategy  to  achieve  MOF  growth  onto  the surface  of  COF  by  covalent  bonds,  namely $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO66@TpPa-1-COF $(\mathrm{Tp}=1,3,5 $ -triformylphloroglucinol, $\mathrm{{Pa}=\ p\cdot}$ - phenylenediamine).  Besides,  Xu  and  co-workers  [141]  prepared $\mathrm{TpPa@UiO-66-(COOH)_{2}}$ hybrid  materials  with  yolk–shellstructured via a template-free solvothermal method. Importantly, a hollow cavity between the UiO-66- $\mathrm{(COOH)}_{2}$ core and the TpPaCOF shell was formed during the transformation of the polyimide shell from amorphous to crystalline COF shell.
Fu and co-workers [71] reported the graft of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ $\mathrm{(DABCO\=\1,4}.$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) and ZIF-8 $\mathrm{(C_{8}H_{12}N_{4}Z n)}$ onto the COF-300 membrane. First, the aqueous solution of acetic acid was added to 1,4-dioxane solution dissolving  terephthalaldehyde  and  tetra-(4-anilyl)-methane,  and the resultant COF-300 mother solution was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days  in  an  autoclave  to  form  COF-300  membrane  on  the polyaniline (PANI)-modified porous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ disk. As-formed COF300  membrane  was  immersed  into  the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ mother solution, which was prepared by mixing $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O,}$ BDC, DABCO in DMF. Finally, the mixture was heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days to obtain [COF-300]- $\mathrm{\cdot}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ composite membrane via the hydrogen bond interaction between BDC linker and  the  dangling $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ of  COF-300.  Similarly,  ZIF-8  was  also successfully  coated  onto  the  surface  of ${\mathrm{COF}}{\cdot}300$ through  the formation of coordination bond between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group to obtain [COF-300]-[ZIF-8] composite membrane.
In addition, Caro group [142] recently presented the example of MOF-in-COF concept, i.e., growth of ultrasmall MOF unit within pores of COF through a two-step immersion method (Fig. 6(a)). As  the  first  step,  the  prepared  TpPa-1  COF  layers  were  fully immersed into the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ methanolic solution for $24\mathrm{h},$ so that ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ was  adsorbed  on  the  pore  wall  of  TpPa-1  through  the coordination  interaction  between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and  the  imine $_\mathrm{~N~}$ sites among TpPa-1. Next, after adding 2-methylimidazole (2-meIM), ultrasmall ZIF-67 unit was rapidly formed within the 1D pores of TpPa-1.  As  confirmed  by  energy  dispersive  X-ray  spectroscopy (EDXS) mapping, successive TpPa-1 layers of approximately $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness were deposited on the surface of ${\tt a}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ substrate, and the  cobalt  signals  from  ZIF-67  were  well  distributed  in  the composite film. The characteristic X-ray diffraction peaks of TpPa1 have been clearly observed from the XRD pattern of ZIF-67-inTpPa-1 membrane, whereas corresponding characteristic peak of ZIF-67 has not been discerned due to its ultrasmall dimension.
Lastly, Wang’s group [143] reported a LZU1-COF $@$ Mn-MOF hybrid material that was formed by interlinking two components at  molecular  level  (Fig  6(b)).  In  detail,  the  obtained  LZU1-COF was  mixed  with $\mathrm{Mn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ and  1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ in ethanol, and the resulting mixture was sealed in a microwave reaction flask and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under microwave irradiation  for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to  produce  the  final  LZU1-COF $@.$ MnMOF.  The  LZU1-COF  integrated  with  Mn-MOF  uniformly thanks to the strong Mn–N coordination interaction. Interestingly,  as-resultant  LZU1-COF@Mn-MOF  hybrid  showed flower-like  morphology,  which  was  beneficial  for  showing enhanced  performance  in  the  lithium  storage.  Besides,  Zou group  [144]  fabricated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{B}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CTF}\mathrm{-}1$ hybrid material by post-synthetic covalent modification method. Firstly, terephthalonitrile and trifluoromethanesulfonic acid were dissolved in chloroform and then heated to $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Upon the subsequent synthesis process, melt $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ salt played the part of  the  reaction  medium  to  assist  the  conversion  of  amorphous CTF-1 into ordered CTF-1. And then by adopting the method of diazonium grafting, B-CTF-1 was functionalized with benzoic acid to  expose  the  carboxyl  group  on  its  surface.  Finally, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti) and B-CTF-1 powder were mixed in dry DMF under an inert  atmosphere,  and  N-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-N'-thylcarbodiimidehydrochloride (EDC·HCl), 1-hydroxybenzotriazole (HOBt), and N,N-diisopropylethylamine (DIPEA) were added to the above dispersion, which was stirred at room temperature for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtained the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{B}$ -CTF-1 composite. The $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)  was  covalently  grafted  onto  CTF-1  by condensation  reaction  to  form  covalent  amide  bonds  for  the construction of hybrid material.
## 3    Versatile applications
The  distinctive  features  of  those  unusual  MOFs,  COFs  and M $\mathrm{)F(\overline{{\alpha}}C O F}$ hybrids  stemming  from  combination  of  the  metal coordination chemistry of MOFs and dynamic covalent chemistry of COFs have endowed them with multifunctionalities for exerting superior performances in adsorption/separation, catalysis, detections, etc.
## 3.1    Adsorption and separation
## 3.1.1    Adsorption
The accessible internal space of porous frameworks confers them great power in molecule adsorptions. Compared with the previous COFs,  the  woven  COF-506(Cu)  show  obvious  advantages  in structural  elasticity  and  reversibility,  and  even  the  adaptive inclusion  ability  towards  guest  molecules.  Yaghi’s  group  [110] tested the molecule adsorption capability of woven COF-506(Cu) and COF-505(Cu). Via using tetrahydrofuran (THF) as a probe molecule,  COF-506(Cu)  exhibited  a  maximum  uptake  of  56.6 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at the relative pressure $\left(P/P_{0}\right)$ of 0.98, while the THF uptake of COF-505(Cu) was ignorable at the same vapor pressure caused by its inaccessible interpenetrated network. What’s more, methyl orange  (MO),  hydroxynaphthol  blue  disodium  salt  (HN)  and methyl blue (MB) with increasing molecular size were selected for further testing. Also, COF-506(Cu) showed remarkable adsorption capacities towards MO and HN (Figs. 7(a) and 7(b)), but  very  small  capacity  towards  MB  due  to  its  larger dimensionality than the intrinsic pore size of COF-506(Cu) (Fig. 7(c)). To one’s surprise, the adsorption uptake towards MB of the demataled  COF-506  was  11.6-fold  higher  than  that  of  pristine COF-506(Cu)  (Fig. 7(d)).  This  virtue  of  COF-506  after demetallation  could  considerably  improve  the  flexibility  and mobility for adaptation and accommodation of MB. Despite lots of progresses achieved, the industrial application of MOFs in the adsorption  field  is  seriously  restricted,  caused  by  their  poor moisture  stability  [145, 146].  Currently,  numerous  of  protocols have emerged in order to arm MOFs with good water resistance [147–150]. For instance, Farha and co-workers [97] reported the introduction of hydrophobic phenylacetylene into NU-401 via the addition  reaction  of  imine  bonds.  As-obtained  NU-401-Q remained an oxygen uptake up to $8.8~\mathrm{mol{\cdot}k g^{-1}}$ comparable to that of  HKUST-1  [151],  but  more  significantly  showed  much improved water resistance for maintaining long-term performance ability. As a proof of concept, more than $75\%$ of the surface area of NU-401-Q  was  retained  while  NU-401  completely  lost  its porosity,  after  immersing  NU-401  and  NU-401-Q  in  deionized water for $20\mathrm{h}$ .
Additionally, a series of MOF@COF hybrid materials emerged and  exhibited  dramatically  improved  adsorption  properties  than isolated MOFs or COFs thanks to the synergistic effect between two  components  [152–154].  Maspoch  and  coworkers  [137] reported  that  the  microspherical $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-TAPBBTCA  hybrid  exhibited  excellent  performance  in  both $\Nu_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ adsorption  uptake.  It  was  reasoned  that  an  amount  of micropores  and  mesopores  were  generated  at  the  interface  of resultant  hybrid  material.  As  verified  by  the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption measurement at 77 K, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@COF-TAPB-BTCA showed a  surface  area  of $1,153\ \mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ ,  approximately  3-fold  higher  of $420\ m^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ of  the  physical  mixture  of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66  and  COFTAPB-BTCA. Moreover, under the same $P/P_{0}$ point, the hybrid showed a water sorption value of $0.26{\bf g\cdot g}^{-1}:$ , more than three times of the physical mixture $(0.07\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . In addition, Feng’s group [21] developed a pore-partitioned materials tpb-pacs CPMs with many exposed  Lewis’s  acid  sites  by  integrating  both  COF  and  MOF merits.  The  gas  adsorption  studies  demonstrated  the  higher adsorption  capacity  of tpb-pacs CPMs  towards  common  gasses compared  with  the  nonpartitioned acs-type  materials  [155, 156].
Among  them,  the  absorption  performance  towards $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ was impressively  high  (0.598 $\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ ),  close  to  the  world-record holders  of  MFM-300  (Al) $\mathrm{\small{[Al_{2}(O H)_{2}(B P T C)(N H_{3})_{6.5}}}$ $\mathrm{\BPTC}\ =$ $^{3,3^{\circ},5,5^{\circ}}$ -biphenyl-tetracar  boxylic  acid] $(0.622\mathrm{\textrm{~}}\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ [157] and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ (B $\mathrm{BTA}=\mathrm{1H},\mathrm{5H}$ -benzo(1,2-d),(4,5-d′)bistriazole) $(0.610\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ [158] at that time.
## 3.1.2    Separation
COFs  are  considered  as  potential  materials  for  gas  separation thanks but not limited to their high porosity, large surface area, and  excellent  thermochemical  stability  [159].  However,  the  too large  pore  size  of  conventional  COFs  is  disadvantageous  for achieving  high  selectivity,  especially  for  sieving  gas  molecules  of small  dimensional  differentiation.  Fortunately,  hybridizations  of MOFs  and  COFs  provide  additional  opportunities  for  pursing enhanced selectivity in applications of gas separation. As a typical example,  Qiu  and  co-workers  [71]  fabricated $\mathrm{COF@MOF}$ composite  membranes  for  the  selective  separation  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas  mixture $\left(\mathrm{Fig~\8(a)}\right)$ .  The  permeability  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas  surpasses that  of  the  other  gases $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right.$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}^{\cdot}$ )  through  the  COF300 membrane, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ membrane, and [COF-300]- $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ composite membrane (Fig. 8(b)). Impressively, the separation factors (SFs) toward the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (1:1) binary  mixture  of  resulting  [COF-300]- $\cdot[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ 1 and  [COF-300]-[ZIF-8]  composite  membranes  were  12.6  and 13.5, respectively, far higher than those of the individual COF-300 (6.0), $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ (7.0) and ZIF-8 (9.1) membranes. The formed  amorphous  MOF  interlayer  was  considered  as  the  key point  for  acquiring  superior  performance,  which  not  only inherited a high gas permeability from the parent crystalline ZIF8,  but  also  tightly  filled  the  cracks  among  COF  grains  for improving separation selectivity. Similarly, Ben reported a [COF300]-[UiO-66]  composite  membrane  [160]  for  separation  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture.  According  to  the  testing  results,  the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity followed the sequence of [COF-300]-[UiO-66] $(17.2)>$ UiO-66 (9.2) $)>\mathrm{COF}{-300}$ (6.0). Moreover, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeability of the [COF-300]-[UiO-66] composite membrane (118,962.3 Barrer) was  also  higher  than  that  of  the  individual  UiO-66  (70,143.6 Barrer) and COF-300 (109,020.3 Barrer) membranes. The classical dissolution–diffusion  mechanism  can  explain  the  superior separation  performance  of  the  composite  membrane  [161]. Namely,  the  pore  sizes  among  composite  membrane  (COF-300 and  UiO-66  were  2  and $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ ,  respectively)  larger  than  the  gas molecules sizes $(\mathrm{H}_{2}=0.289\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}=0.33\ \mathrm{nm},$ ) ensured the rapid pervasion of these gas molecules. Besides, the much lower isosteric adsorption enthalpies (Qst) towards $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ 1 $\mathrm{COF}{-}300\approx3{-}7\$ $\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and  UiO- $66\approx6{-}8\ \mathrm{kJ{\cdot}m o l{\cdot}l}^{\mathrm{v}},$ compared  to  that  towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{COF}–300~\approx~32–33~\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and $\mathrm{UiO}–66\approx25\mathrm{\kJ}–\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ favored the high $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity.
Moreover, Caro and co-workers [142] fabricated novel MOF-inCOF  membrane  for  gas  separation  via  restricting  the  growth  of ZIF-67  unit  inside  pores  of  TpPa-1  layers.  Gas-separation performance  was  measured  by  placing  the  MOF-in-COF membrane into a home-made module with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ as sweep gas (Fig. $8(\mathrm{c}))$ ). As-resultant ZIF-67-in-TpPa-1 membrane showed superior permeability toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(3,800\mathrm{GPU})$ ) than those of the other gases $\mathrm{(CO_{2},C H_{4},C_{3}H_{6},}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ was about 97, 99, 31, 18, respectively) (Fig. 8(d)). In addition, the real mixed-gas SFs of the ZIF-67-inTpPa-1 membrane for equimolar $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ gas  pairs  could  reach  34.9,  33.3,  110.5,  and  192.7, respectively,  much  higher  than  that  of  pure  TpPa-1-based membrane. To prove the universality of proposed concept, other MOF-in-COF types of membranes like ZIF-8-in-TpPa-1 and ZIF67-in-TpBD  were  also  prepared  and  showed  superior $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation  performance  than  isolated  MOF-  or/and  COF-based membranes.
Alternatively,  adding $\mathrm{MOF@COF}$ fillers  into  traditional polymer matrix to form mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) can effectively improve the gas separation performance than pristine polymers. Zhao’s group [136] reported that UiO- $\mathsf{\Omega}_{6-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{Pa}-}$ 1  based  MMMs  showed  enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability  and $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity  than  commercial  polysulfone  (PSf).  It  was suggested that the TpPa-1 shell featured with large pore $(18.3\mathrm{~\AA})$ constructed fast gas permeation channels in the resultant MMMs. Moreover, both the good compatibility and dispersibility of TpPa1  shell  were  feasibly  realized  owing  to  their  similar  organic compositions  to  the  PSf  matrix,  which  were  favorable  for acquiring high separation selectivity. As expected, the size-sieving MOF  pores  endowed  UiO-66-NH2@TpPa-1/PSf  MMMs  with  a high $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity of 46.7, which had an increment of $79\%$ than  the  26.1  of  pure  PSf  membrane.  The  UiO- $.66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{Pa}\mathrm{-}$ 1/PSf  MMMs  also  maintained  excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability  and $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity after a successive $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ separation testing of $^{100\mathrm{~h},}$ which  was  attributed  to  the  high  chemical  stability  of added  UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Tp}\mathrm{Pa}{\mathrm{-}}1$ fillers  as  well  as  their  strong interfacial adhesion to the PSf matrix.
## 3.2    Catalysis
## 3.2.1    Photocatalysis
As a class of porous crystalline materials with molecular tunability and conjugated skeletons, COFs are a type of promising candidate for  photocatalysis  applications  [162, 163].  However,  their photocatalytic  activity  is  hampered  due  to  their  rare  active  sites and  inherent  high  exciton  binding  energy  [164, 165].  MOFs,  by virtue of the abundance of active site and semiconductor-like band structure,  are  considered  as  another  nominator  but  suffer  from spontaneous  and  fast  exciton  quench  [166–172].  By  combining dynamic covalent chemistry of COFs with coordination chemistry of MOFs, novel MOFs, COFs as well as MOF@COF hybrids have been  developed  in  the  applications  of  photodegradation  of pollutants,  hydrogen  generation,  and  organic  transformation reactions [28, 76, 77, 133, 173].
As a pioneering work, Zhang’s group reported [27] that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL- $68@^{\prime}$ TPA-COF core–shell hybrid materials for photocatalytic degradation of rhodamine B (Rh B). Under visible light irradiation $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm},$ , the ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectra demonstrated the intensity of the typical absorption peak of Rh B at around $554~\mathrm{nm}$ decreased with time in the existence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68@TPA-COF (Fig. 9(a)). Moreover, the hybrid material had a higher degradation rate constant $(0.077~\mathrm{{min}^{-1})}$ than pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-68 $\mathrm{(0.054~min^{-1})}$ )  (Fig. 9(b)).  According  to  the $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms  and  Kubelka-Munk  function  results,  the  superior catalytic  degradation  performance  of  the  hybrid  materials  was mainly resulted from the increased surface area $(539\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ as well as  the  lowered  band  gap $(2.21~\mathrm{eV})$ of  the  hybrid  materials.  In addition, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TTF}$ -TTA hybrid material with covalent  heterojunction  showing  enhanced  photocatalytic  ability for the decomposition of the methyl orange (MO) and colorless phenol  was  reported  by  Cai’ s  group  [174].  Using  MO  as  the example, the rate constant of MO photodegradation followed the sequence  of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{TTB}–\mathrm{TT}$ A $(0.20\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})\ >$ TTBTTA $(0.086\mathrm{\min^{-1}})>\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -MIL-125(Ti) $(0.021\mathrm{{min}^{-1})}$ . It suggested that the efficient charge transfer from the conduction band of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-125(Ti) to the valence band of TTB-TTA was determinant for  the  superior  performance.  That  was,  electrons  in  the conduction band of TTB-TTA were captured by $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ for oxidation of MO while holes remaining in the valence band of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)  could  react  with $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ to  generate  OH·  for further  degradation  of  MO.  Moreover,  thanks  to  the  robust stability of TTB-TTA COF serving as a protection shell, NH2-MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})\textcircled{\alpha}$ TTB-TTA maintained the photocatalytic conversion no less  than $85.1\%$ after  4  successive  photocatalytic  tests.  Besides, Peng and co-workers [175] studied the photocatalytic activity of IR-MOF3 $@$ COF-LZU1 hybrid material for the degradation of pnitrophenol  (PNP).  Under  the  irradiation  of  visible  light,  IR
MOF3 $@$ COF-LZU  completely  degraded  the  yellow  PNP  after only $3.5\ \mathrm{h},$ which  surpassed  the  degradation  rate  of  most conventional photocatalysts [176, 177].
Photocatalytic  water  splitting  using  solar  energy  is  a  very promising way to produce clean hydrogen energy [178–180] and photoactive materials based on MOF@COF hybrid materials have recently  emerged  as  efficient  photocatalysts.  Lan  and  coworkers [28]  grafted $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66  onto  TpPa-1-COFs  via  the  covalent bonds  and  utilized  the  resultant $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}U i O{-}66@T p P a{-}1{-}C O F}$ hybrid material for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. After composition optimization, the hydrogen evolution rate reached its maximum $(23.41\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}})$ via  using $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@TpPa-1-COF  (4:6, molar  ratio)  as  the  photocatalyst,  which  was  nearly  20  times higher than the sole TpPa-1-COF $(1.22\mathrm{mmol{g}^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}^{\cdot},$ ). The catalytic mechanism  based  on  the  DFT  calculation  and  TEM  results indicated  that  the  photogenerated  electrons  were  rapidly transferred  from  the  valence  band  of  TpPa-1-COF  to  the conduction  band  of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66  with  the  assistance  of conjugated $-C=\Nu-$ bonds. Consequently, the separated electrons in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 efficiently reduced $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with the assistance of Pt  co-catalyst,  and  holes  remaining  at  the  TpPa-1-COF  were caught by sodium ascorbate for accomplishing the catalytic cycle smoothly.  Also,  Zou’s  group  [144]  prepared  a  benzoic  acidmodified  covalent  triazine-based  framework  (B-CTF-1),  which was further integrated with ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}{\mathrm{-}}125({\mathrm{Ti}})$ via covalent amide bonds in order to purse improved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate. Thanks to the  amide  covalent  bonds  playing  a  charge-mediating  role  for promoted  charger  transfer  process, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/B-CTF-1 hybrid  exerted  a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution  rate  up  to $360\ \upmu\mathrm{molcdoth^{-1}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under visible light irradiation, which was over two-fold higher of that of the sole CTF-1. Moreover, $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125\mathrm{(Ti)/B}\mathrm{-CTF}\mathrm{-}1$ maintained good catalytic activity after four recycle photocatalysis.
Besides, Jiang’s group [181] constructed $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@TFPTDETH $(\mathrm{TFPT}=1,3,5\$ -tris-(4-formyl-phenyl) triazine, and DETH $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\ 2\AA,5\$ -diethoxybenzene-1,4-dicarbohydrazide)  hetero-framework for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. By varying the amount of TFPT (0.013,  0.026,  0.052  and $0.078\ \mathrm{mmol};$ )  and  DETH  (0.019,  0.038, $0.076,$ and $0.114\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ),  a  series  of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66@TFPT-DETH samples were prepared and denoted as $\operatorname{U}\ @\operatorname{TDEn}$ $(n=1,2,4$ and
6), respectively. Thanks to the broadened visible light absorption and  boosted  charge  transfer  rate  of  the  hetero-framework, $\mathrm{U@{TDE}}$ presented low interfacial charge transfer resistance and large  photocurrent  density.  Among  them, $\mathrm{U@TDE4}$ with  an optimized thickness of $20.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ exerted the maximum hydrogen evolution rate $(7178\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ , while pristine TFPT-DETH was only $2301\mathrm{mmol{g}^{-1}{b}^{-1}}$ $(\operatorname{Fig}9(\mathsf{c}))$ ). Moreover, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO- $66@$ TFPT$\mathsf{D P P(U@T D P4)}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66@^{{\prime}}$ TFPT-DMTH $(\mathrm{U@TDM4})$ were also synthesized via varying the side chains among COF shell from the original TFPT-DETH to TFPT-DETH-methoxy, TFPTDPP-propoxy $(\mathrm{DPP}=2,5\$ -dipropoxy-1,4-dihydrazide) and TFPTDMTH-ethoxy  (2,5-dimethoxyterephthalohydrazide).  The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate ranked as the sequence of $\mathrm{U@TDE4>U@TDP4>}$ $\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{TDM}4$ (Fig. 9(d)),  owing  to  the  decreased  interlayer  charge transfer  efficiencies  arising  from  the  side  chain  weakened $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and aggregation [182, 183].
Photo-triggered organic transformation via combining chemistry of MOFs and COFs is also an intriguing research topic due to the improved photocatalytic activity and product selectivity originating  from  the  synergistic  effect.  For  example,  MOF-901 [20] composed of unusual hexameric titanium oxo $\mathrm{(Ti_{6}O_{6})}$ metal node  and  benzene-1,4-dialdehyde  linker,  demonstrates  high performance  in  photocatalytic  polymerization  of  methyl methacrylate  (MMA)  under  visible-light  irradiation.  MOF-901 afforded polyMMA in the yield of $87\%$ , molecular weight (Mn) of $26,850\ \mathrm{g{\cdotmol^{-1}}}$ and  corresponding  polydispersity  index  (PDI)  of 1.6, which is much superior to the commercially available Degussa $\mathrm{P}{-}25\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (yield $=52\%$ , $M_{\mathrm{n}}=17{,}000\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1};$ , $\mathrm{PDI}=1.6\$ ), and UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $^{13\%}$ $18{,}500\ \mathrm{g{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ , 1.6). What’s more, MOF-902 [86] was  composed  of  a  broader  conjugation  skeleton  and  therefore possessed a lower band gap energy $(2.50\mathrm{eV})$ than MOF-901 (2.65 eV), which was favorable for harvesting light energy. When using MOF-902  as  the  photocatalyst,  the  produced  PMMA  had characteristics of a higher $M_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(31,465\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ), higher yield $(84\%)$ , and much lower PDI (1.11). Moreover, MOF-902 was applicable for photo-catalyzing the self-polymerization of benzyl methacrylate (BMA) and styrene (St) monomers. The produced polyBMA presented a $M_{\mathrm{n}}$ of $32,050\ \mathrm{g{\cdotmol^{-1}}}$ and PDI of 1.11, while the produced polySt gave a Mn of $6660\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and PDI of 1.65.
## 3.2.2    Thermocatalysis
Knoevenagel condensation reaction is widely used in the synthesis of  coumarin,  cosmetic  and  pharmaceutical  intermediates,  which can be satisfactorily catalyzed by materials containing basic sites (e.g., $\scriptstyle-C=\Nu-$ ) and accessible pore structures.
Murugavel’s  group  [94]  reported  a  novel  imine-linked polycrystalline  zinc  phosphate  framework  for  Knoevenagel reactions  between  4-nitrobenzaldehyde  and  malononitrile.  The reactants could be completely converted at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after reaction of $20\mathrm{{h}},$ , thanks to rich basic $-C=\Nu-$ sites and accessible pores of the constructed framework. In addition, by taking advantage of lability of the dynamic imine bond, introduction of the diamine fragment into  PCN-164  framework  was  achieved  by  Zhou’s  group  for further tuning the basicity [102]. For instance, the phenylenediamine  fragments  of  PCN-164  frameworks  were successfully  exchanged  to  cyclohexane-1,4-diamine  (CHDA)  or 2,5-dimethylbenzene-1,4-diamine  (DBDA)  by  utilizing  reversible dissociation/reassociation  of  imine  bonds  (Fig. 10(a)).  Using CHDA-exchanged  PCN-164  for  demonstration,  its  catalytic conversion for Knoevenagel reactions between 4- nitrobenzaldehyde  and  malononitrile  was  improved  to $99\%$ , higher than the $58\%$ of pristine PCN-164.
As one of the widely used raw materials for the Knoevenagel condensation  reaction,  aromatic  aldehydes  are  prone  to  be oxidized  in  priority  under  aerobic  environment.  To  solve  this problem,  Gao  and  co-workers  [135]  designed  a  PCN-222- Co@TpPa-1  hybrid  material  featuring  both  Lewis  acid  and Bronsted base active sites for the one-pot cascade deacetalizationKnoevenagel condensation reaction. In details, (dimethoxymethyl)- benzene was first transformed into benzaldehyde by the Lewis acid $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ clusters  and $\operatorname{Co}(\mathrm{II})$ centers  exposed  by  the  PCN-222-Co core. Subsequently, the $-C=\Nu-$ dangling on TpPa-1 shell acted as Bronsted  base  site  to  catalyze  the  conversion  of  benzaldehyde intermediate  and  malononitrile  to  2-benzylidenemalononitrile product.  Impressively,  PCN-222-Co@TpPa-1  showed  a  high conversion up to $99.3\%$ at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ as well as outstanding recyclability.
It has been previously reported that the surface and channel of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe)  are  both  hydrophilic  [184].  So  that  for hydrophobic substrates like styrene, the application of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL101(Fe) in its catalytic conversion is seriously limited. Li and coworkers  [134]  coated  a  hydrophobic  NTU-COF  shell  on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-101(Fe) to obtain $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Fe})@\mathrm{NTU}$ hybrid material by a one-step method. In the oxidation of styrene, the as-designed $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe)@NTU hybrid showed higher conversion $(32\%)$ and  selectivity $(84\%)$ than  sole $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 $24\%$ conversion and $27\%$ selectivity)  and  NTU  COF $10\%$ conversion  and $67\%$ selectivity) contributed by the synergistic effect between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL
101(Fe) and NTU-COF. Namely, NTU-COF shell promoted the mass  transfer  of  hydrophobic  styrene,  while $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101  (Fe) provided  coordinated  unsaturated  catalytic  sites  for  efficiently conversion of incoming styrene. The hybridization of MOFs and COFs  not  only  inherits  their  original  features  but  also  presents synergistic effect beyond the individual component to improve the performance  for  various  functional  applications.  For  example, Zhao’s  group  [29]  designed  a  sandwiched  catalytic  Pd/UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{COF}{-}1$ platform  for  hydrogenation  of  olefins  including cyclohexene,  trans-stilbene,  and  triphenylethylene  (Fig. 10(b)). $\mathrm{Pd/UiO-66-NH_{2}}@\mathrm{COF-3d}$ with COF-1 shell thickness of $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ and pore size of $2.5\mathrm{nm}$ was used as a catalytic testing example. As the molecular sizes of cyclohexene $(5.39\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.02\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and transstilbene $(11.65\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.06\mathrm{~\AA~})$ were significantly smaller than that of the pore size of COF-1, the $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{COF}{-}3\mathrm{d}$ composite exhibited a yield of $98.8\%$ for cyclohexene and $87.1\%$ for transstilbene. Distinctively, triphenylethylene ( $0.71\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ that  was  difficult  to  be  diffused  through  the  COF-1  shell  of $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{COF}{-}3\mathrm{d}$ was  negligible  converted  (Fig. 10(c)). The  synergy  between  the  three  type  components  among sandwiched $\mathrm{Pd/UiO–66–NH_{2}@C O F–1}$ endowed  them  with  high size-selective  catalytic  performance:  Namely,  Pd  nanoparticles served  as  catalytically  active  sites; $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ core  acted  as porous support, and COF-1 shell played a size-selective regulator role.
## 3.2.3    Electrocatalysis
Wang’s  group  [82]  prepared  highly  dispersed $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ onto  Ndoped porous carbon (NPC) via the calcination of ZIF- $67@\mathrm{BFC}$ hybrid materials (ZIF-67 = zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, BFC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzoic  acid  modified  covalent  organic  framework).  By increasing  the  BFC  content  from  0.25  to  1  g, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}.1$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-2$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-3$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}\mathrm{-}4$ samples  were obtained  respectively,  and  then  tested  as  the  oxygen  reduction reaction  (ORR)  catalysts.  Among  them,  the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-2$ exerted a smaller Tafel slope (79 mV·decade−1) and higher mass activity of $130\mathrm{\A{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ at the overpotential of $400~\mathrm{{mV}_{:}}$ , compared to those of other $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@/\mathrm{NPCs}$ $92~\mathrm{mV}$ ·decade−1 and $5.3~\mathrm{A{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC-1}$ , $101\mathrm{mV}$ ·decade−1 and $1.9\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-3.$ , 83  mV·decade−1 and $4.7\mathrm{\A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}{-4}$ ,  and  93 mV·decade−1 and  7.9 $\mathbf{A\cdotg}^{-1}$ for  pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ ).  The  superior performance  acquired  by $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-2$ was  ascribed  to  its higher  specific  surface  area $(228.0\ \mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ than  those  of  other $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPCs}$ $(7.62~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-1$ , $147.11\ \mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}-3$ and $110.85\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NPC}{-4},$ ).
Yamauchi  and  co-workers  [78]  prepared  a  highly  graphited nitrogen-doped porous carbon electrocatalyst (GC@COF-NC, GC $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ graphitic  carbon, ${\mathrm{NC}}=$ porous  carbon)  which  exhibited  high activity in ORR testing. The formation of GC@COF-NC hybrid materials  followed  the  below  processes:  Firstly,  ZIF-67  was carbonized  to  form  GC;  secondly,  the  as-prepared  GC  was introduced into the synthetic process of DAAQ-TFPCOF $(\mathrm{DAAQ}\:=\:2,6\$ -diaminoanthraquinone,  and $\mathrm{TFP}\ =\ 1,3,5-$ triformylphloroglucinol) to make COF grow homogeneously on the GC surface; finally, the as-prepared ${\mathrm{GC}}@{\mathrm{COF}}$ was thermally converted  to $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}$ materials  through  carbonization under  inert  nitrogen  atmosphere.  The ${\mathrm{GC}}@{\mathrm{COF}}$ with  different concentrations of COF monomer (0.01, 0.04, and $0.08~\mathrm{mM}$ ) was pyrolyzed  at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to  obtain  the $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.01},$ ${\mathrm{GC@COF}}.$ $\mathrm{NC}_{0.04}$ and $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.08},$ respectively.  According  to  ORR measurement  results,  the  oxygen  reduction  characteristic  peak intensity followed the sequence of $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.04}$ $\mathrm{(0.796~V)}>$ $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.01}$ $(0.793{\mathrm{~V}})>$ pure  GC $(0.765\mathrm{~V})>\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF}-$ $\mathrm{NC}_{0.08}$ (0.762 V). Moreover, the onset potential $\mathrm{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}E_{\mathrm{onset}},$ calculated when  the  current  density  reached $5\%$ of  the  limiting  current density) and half-wave potential $(E_{1/2})$ both obeyed the following sequence: $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.04}$ ( $\cdot E_{\mathrm{onset}}=0.923\mathrm{~V~}$ and $E_{1/2}=0.8441\mathrm{~V})$ ) $>\mathrm{GC@COF-NC_{0.01}}$ (0.913  and  0.837  V) $>$ pure  GC  (0.912  and $0.830\mathrm{~V})\mathrm{~>~GC@COF–NC_{0.08}}$ (0.874  and  0.811  V),  further demonstrating the superior ORR activity of $\mathrm{GC}@\mathrm{COF-NC}_{0.04}.$
## 4    Other applications
## 4.1 Sensing
Zhang  and  co-workers  [139]  designed  a  novel  Co-MOF@TPNCOF  (TPN $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ terephthalonitrile)  hybrid  nanosheet  for  constructing a sensitive bioplatform of aptasensor used for detection of ampicillin (AMP) residing in human serum, river water, and milk. The  Co-MOF  [185]  reacted  by $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ with  2- methylimidazole in  situ grew  on  the  TPN-COF  surface,  thus forming Co-MOF $@$ TPN-COF composite. According to electrochemical  impedance  spectroscopy  (EIS),  the  tested impedance  obeyed  the  following  sequence:  bare  Au  electrodes (AE)  (91  Ω)  <  Co-MOF@TPN-COF/AE $(105\Omega)\ <$ Apt (aptamer) $/C o-M O F@\mathrm{TPN-COF/AE}$ (447 $\Omega^{\cdot}$ ) $\prec$ AMP (ampicillin)/Apt/Co-MOF $@^{\prime}$ TPN-COF/AE  (910  Ω).  Also,  CoMOF@TPN-COF  exhibited  a  larger  charge-transfer  resistance $\left(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\right)$ value $(463~\Omega)$ than  sole  TPN-COF $(175\ \Omega)$ and  Co-MOF (147  Ω).  Co-MOF $@$ TPN-COF-based  AMP  aptasensor  showed high sensitivity for detection of AMP at very wide concentration ranges (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1,000, and $2{,}000\ \mathrm{pg{\cdot}m L^{-1}},$ ). The excellent sensing properties of Co-MOF $@$ TPN-COF-based AMP aptasensor  might  be  attributed  by  following  factors:  i)  The synergistic  effect  between  the  two  components  and  the $\pi\cdot$ conjugated  structure  endowed  the  hybrid  material  with  high electrochemical activity; ii) the aptamer strand could be anchored on the Co-MOF@TPN-COF composite through the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and hydrogen bonds; iii) the high specific surface area of Co-MOF@TPN-COF was not only conducive to the immobilization  of  the  aptamer  but  also  enhanced  the electrochemical signal.
Antigen galectin-3 (GL-3) was used not only as an indicator of cardiovascular  risk,  but  also  a  prognostic  factor  for  predicting cancer progression [186]. Yola and co-workers [187] developed a new  convenient  and  effective  amperometric  method  for  antigen GL-3 detection. They designed an amperometric immunosensor based on specific antibody–antigen interactions via combination of  gold  nanoparticle-functionalized  graphitic  carbon  nitride nanosheets $\langle\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{Au}$ NPs)  with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TPBA}–$ TPA $\mathrm{{TPA}=}$ terephthalaldehyde). $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}@A u}$ NPs  component with good conductivity was chosen as the sensing site, which was available  for  immobilizing  the  antibody1-GL-3  (anti-GL-3- $-\mathrm{Ab}_{1}$ ) through  amino-gold  affinity; $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) $@$ TPBA-TPA ensured acquirement of stable and sensitive amperometric signals based on the electrostatic and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions with the antibody2- GL-3  (anti-GL- $3\mathrm{-Ab}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ).  The  immunosensor  exhibited  a  wide linearity  range $(0.0001{-}20.0\ \mathrm{ng{\cdot}m L^{-1}})$ and  a  low  detection  limit (LOD) $(0.025\mathrm{\pg{\cdot}m L^{-1}})$ ). Moreover, the immunosensors demonstrated high selectivity for GL-3 recognition in five different solutions  including  standard  GL-3,  GL-3/glucose  (GLU),  GL3/dopamine  (DOP),  GL-3/L-threonine  (THR)  and  GL-3/bovine serum  albumin  (BSA).  In  brief,  the  stable  and  environmentally friendly immunosensor exhibited the advantages of convenience, rapid response and low-cost for antigen GL-3 detection, compared with  traditional  analytical  techniques.  Obviously,  MOF/COF hybrid materials have shown great potential in detection [140, 188, 189].
## 4.2    Energy storage
Recently,  Wang  and  co-workers  [143]  reported  COF/Mn-MOF hybrid  materials  and  their  derivate $\begin{array}{r l}{\operatorname{MnS}_{\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{NS-C}}\end{array}$ composites  for lithium storages. When being tested as the negative electrode for lithium-ion  batteries,  as-resultant ${\mathrm{COF/Mn–MOF}}$ showed  a capacity of $1{,}015\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ after 650 cycles, larger than that of the sole  Mn-MOFs $(258\ \mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}})$ )  and  COFs $(126\mathrm{\mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}})$ .  In addition, $\mathrm{MnS@NS-C-g}$ hollow $(1,068\mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1}})$ and $\mathrm{MnS}@\mathrm{NS-C-}$ l  core–shell $(1,017\mathrm{~mAh~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ )  microspheres,  the  derivatives  of COF/Mn-MOF  synthesized  in  the  gas-phase  and  liquid-phase, respectively, showed greatly increased lithium-storage capabilities, attributed to the greatly improved conductivity and doping of N and S active sites.
Supercapacitors, as a promising type of energy storage device have attracted increasing attentions. Samorì and co-workers [130] reported a supercapacitor electrode fabricated based on the azaMOF@COF  hybrid  material.  According  to  electrochemical characterizations,  the  areal  capacitance  of  the  supercapacitors followed  the  sequence  of  aza- $\operatorname{\mathrm{.MOF@COF}}$ $(20.35~\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})~>$ MOF@COF-LZU1 $9.56~\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}})~>~\mathrm{MOF{-}U i O{-}66{\-}N H_{2}}$ (7.89 $\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ . Moreover, the corresponding stack capacitance of azaMOF@COF was calculated as $1.16~\mathrm{F{\cdot}c m^{-3}}$ , which was nearly two times  higher  than  those  of  pure $\mathrm{MOF–UiO–66–NH_{2}}$ and MOF $@$ COF-LZU1  materials.  Importantly,  the  extended $\pi\cdot$ - delocalization  and  decreased  pore  size  (from  ca. $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ to  ca. $0.7\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ ) in aza-MOF $@$ COF were suggested to play a critical role in enhancing the performance of the supercapacitor.
## 5    Conclusion and prospects
This  review  has  thoroughly  described  the  development  of  novel porous  organic  frameworks  via  combination  of  the  covalent chemistry  of  COFs  and  coordination  chemistry  of  MOFs.  For examples,  the  imine  and  B–O  bonds  popularly  employed  in linking COFs have been introduced to construct unusual MOFs, which  are  formidable  to  be  obtained  through  the  conventional metal–ligand  coordination.  With  the  aid  of  directional coordination  bond,  meanwhile,  novel  woven  frameworks  have been successfully obtained in good crystallinity. Impressively, after removal  of  metal  species,  as-obtained  metal-free  woven frameworks have exhibited dynamic and adaptive abilities toward incoming substrates. Moreover, MOF and COF hybrids have also been  extensively  exploited  for  integration  of  both  advantages  of the  individual  components.  Such  colorful  chemistry  nature  of aforementioned  porous  materials  has  also  endowed  them  with multifunctionalities required for exhibiting superior performances in  versatile  application  fields  including  adsorption,  separation, catalysis,  biosensing  and  energy  storage.  Nevertheless,  there  are still  lots  of  challenges  remaining  to  be  resolved  in  this  nascent research field and hence the corresponding perspectives are also provided herein on basis of our personal insights.
(1)  Dynamic  covalent  bonds  bring  new  opportunity  for construction  of  novel  MOFs  especially  whose  secondary  metal nodes  are  incompatible  with  the  conventional  MOF  synthesis conditions.  However,  current  successful  examples  are  merely constructed  via  formation  of  the  typical  imine  and  B–O  bonds, seriously impeding the expansion of MOFs scope as well as their further multifunctionality. In fact, other covalent bonds including amide  (CO–N),  carbon–nitrogen $(\mathrm{C-N})$ ,  alkene $(\mathrm{C=C})$ ,  and azodioxide $(\Nu{=}\Nu)$ ) have already been adopted for construction of various  types  of  COFs,  which  are  also  potentially  workable  for MOFs  explorations  through  judicious  design  and  selection  of functionalized metal precursors and organic ligands.
(2) Similar challenges exist in the synthesis protocols of MOF and  COF  hybrids,  in  which  the  hybridization  procedure  is  also limited  by  the  typical  imine  linkage  between  MOFs  and  COFs. Hence,  other  covalent  bonds-based  linkages  are  also  waiting  for being  adopted  for  hybridizing  MOFs  and  COFs.  Moreover,  the coordination induced interlinkage [143] proposed by Wang et. al. is  very  promising  to  be  promoted  for  universal  construction  of MOF  and  COF  hybrids,  as  the  unsaturated  coordination  metal centers  and  electron  pair  donating  organic  groups  are  easily available from MOFs and COFs components, respectively. Hence, more  successful  novel  MOF  and  COF  hybrid  examples  are foreseeable in the near future.
(3)  It  is  still  formidable  to  well  control  the  morphology  and structure  of  MOF  and  COF  hybrids  due  to  the  very  little knowledge of the formation mechanism and mutual interaction. In-situ tools  (e.g.,  operando  X-ray  diffraction  and  cryotransmission electron microscopy) are very useful for monitoring the  formation  kinetics  and  low-dose  transmission  electron microscopy  is  crucial  for  observing  the  interface  of  heterocomponent at molecular or even atomic scale, which may in turn provide  theory  principles  for  guiding  controllable  and  subtle synthesis of MOF and COF hybrids.
(4)  Although  lots  of  achievements  have  been  made  for  MOF and COF hybrids in application fields of adsorption, separation, catalysis, it is still in its infancy and even virgin stage like in the fields of biomaterials and optoelectronic devices. Apart from the efforts  devoted  to  development  of  emerging  synthesis  protocols, fundamentally studying the characters of each component as well as  exploration  of  exceptional  properties  stemming  from  the synergistic effect between inter-components is urgently desirable, not only in order to optimize their performance but also greatly expand their application scope.
Before ending, we hope the current challenges will encourage and  trigger  much  more  efforts  to  be  paid  on  this  emerging research field. With increasing quests from all over the world, we are  confident  that  novel  porous  materials  combining  chemistry characts of both MOFs and COFs will have a bright prospect in the incoming future.
